339
00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:53.840
Samuel Smith: Even though you use a Diffie-Hellman key exchange using

340
00:42:53.900 --> 00:43:09.220
Samuel Smith: Key pairs, you… you're using the Diffie-Hellman to create a shared secret, so… so the security is based on shared secret. It's a very weak form of authentication. That also applies to Signal, everybody who uses a Signal app that's using shared secret authentication.

341
00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:25.480
Samuel Smith: That applies to VPNs. They do the same thing, they're using a shared secret encryption. It's called, AuthCrypt, is what the people tend to call it. So all of those are subject to various attacks, including a key compromise impersonation attack.

342
00:43:25.950 --> 00:43:32.339
Samuel Smith: All of passwords, username and passwords, those are shared secrets.

343
00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:38.220
Samuel Smith: So every system that uses a username and password as a form of authentication, as a shared secret.

344
00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:47.360
Samuel Smith: all of, well, except for rare exceptions, all of OAuth, OIDC, those bearer tokens are shared secrets.

345
00:43:47.500 --> 00:43:53.680
Samuel Smith: so, and what has happened is, is that…

346
00:43:53.770 --> 00:44:06.059
Samuel Smith: But most of those… all of those systems are based on the idea of a secure channel. So you… you create a secure channel with an interactive handshake, and then you assume that…

347
00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:16.189
Samuel Smith: anything you do on that channel is protected, right? So you have a protected session, and therefore you can exchange shared secrets over protected session.

348
00:44:16.190 --> 00:44:28.559
Samuel Smith: And have a reasonable assumption that those secrets won't leak out, which, of course, is an unreasonable assumption, because you have two parties that are both reputable, they both had plausible deniability.

349
00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:33.710
Samuel Smith: With regard to those shared secrets, and that, starts the path of,

350
00:44:33.850 --> 00:44:51.150
Samuel Smith: various exploits, primarily what is called a recursive privilege escalation attack exploits, which now, after several years… I remember 4 or 5 years ago, that was sort of like, oh, this is a new attack that people are learning how to do, right? It was like, here's this new attack.

351
00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:57.740
Samuel Smith: And now, it is baked in. It's like, you know, everybody knows how to do it in Mythos, for example.

352
00:44:57.900 --> 00:45:03.740
Samuel Smith: And any AI that's trained to do it is able to discover, you know, attacks

353
00:45:03.920 --> 00:45:19.720
Samuel Smith: that are basically just discovering, you know, recursive privilege escalation vectors to attack things. So shared secrets are inherently vulnerable to recursive privilege escalation attacks, because the secret is shared. So there's two ends.

354
00:45:19.800 --> 00:45:41.869
Samuel Smith: And if one end is compromised, then the other end is compromised. And then that means that you just went across the boundary from one end to the other end, and now you're in the other end, you've moved horizontally or vertically, and now you do another shared secret attack to move horizontally or vertically through the infrastructure, and you just keep going until you get to where you want to go. So you start at the weakest edge, and work your way through the system.

355
00:45:41.870 --> 00:45:45.200
Samuel Smith: And since there's always a weakest edge, it's always weak.

356
00:45:45.200 --> 00:45:51.710
Samuel Smith: There's always grandma out there who doesn't protect your shared secrets, or grandpa who doesn't protect your shared secrets.

357
00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:53.620
Samuel Smith: Nothing wrong with that.

358
00:45:53.620 --> 00:45:54.540
